-- title:
--   Two minimal truncated presentations differ by an automorphism
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field and let $C$ be a commutative local $k$-algebra that is finite as a $k$-module. Fix natural numbers $e$ and $M$, write $\mathfrak{t}_0 = \ker(\mathrm{constantCoeff} : k[X_0,\dots,X_{e-1}] \to k)$ for the ideal generated by the variables in the polynomial ring $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}\ (\mathrm{Fin}\ e)\ k$, let $T = k[X_0,\dots,X_{e-1}]/\mathfrak{t}_0^{M+1}$ be the corresponding truncated polynomial algebra, and let $\mathfrak{t}$ denote the image of $\mathfrak{t}_0$ in $T$ under the quotient map. Let $\alpha, \beta : T \to C$ be two $k$-algebra homomorphisms, both assumed surjective, and assume that $\ker \alpha \subseteq \mathfrak{t}^2$ and $\ker \beta \subseteq \mathfrak{t}^2$. The assertion is that there exists a $k$-algebra automorphism $\theta$ of $T$ such that the composite of $\theta$ with $\beta$ equals $\alpha$, i.e. $\beta \circ \theta = \alpha$ as $k$-algebra homomorphisms $T \to C$.
--
--   This is the comparison step showing that a minimal presentation of a finite local $k$-algebra by a truncated polynomial algebra is unique up to an automorphism of the presenting algebra, the artinian, completion-free form of the statement that being a complete intersection is independent of the chosen presentation. It is proved using the truncated inverse function theorem [`MvPolynomial.bijective_algHom_truncated_of_forall_exists_sub_mem_sq`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.bijective_algHom_truncated_of_forall_exists_sub_mem_sq), and is used by [`Algebra.exists_ker_eq_span_of_surjective_truncated_of_ker_eq_span`](thm.html#Algebra.exists_ker_eq_span_of_surjective_truncated_of_ker_eq_span) to transport a description of the kernel of one presentation to another.
